delegatecall executes another contract's code, but using the CALLER's storage, msg.sender, and msg.value. It's the mechanism behind proxy/upgradeable contracts -- and also a common source of catastrophic bugs when storage layouts don't match.

EXAMPLE:
contract Proxy {
    address public implementation;
    address public owner; // storage slot 1 -- must match Logic's layout!

    function upgrade(address newImpl) external {
        require(msg.sender == owner);
        implementation = newImpl;
    }

    fallback() external payable {
        (bool ok, ) = implementation.delegatecall(msg.data);
        require(ok, "Delegatecall failed");
    }
}

WHY IT MATTERS: If the Proxy and Logic contracts declare state variables in a different order, delegatecall will read/write the WRONG storage slots -- this exact class of bug has caused real losses (e.g. the 2017 Parity multisig freeze, ~$150M+ locked). Never use delegatecall against untrusted or mismatched contracts.

FURTHER READING:
- Solidity docs, delegatecall: https://docs.soliditylang.org/en/latest/introduction-to-smart-contracts.html#delegatecall-callcode-and-libraries
- OpenZeppelin, Proxy patterns: https://docs.openzeppelin.com/upgrades-plugins/proxies

