'Push' payments (the contract sends ETH to users automatically) can fail and block your whole contract if even one recipient's address can't accept ETH. 'Pull' payments let users withdraw their own balance whenever they want -- much safer.

EXAMPLE:
mapping(address => uint256) public pendingWithdrawals;

function creditWinner(address winner, uint256 amount) internal {
    pendingWithdrawals[winner] += amount; // just record it, don't send yet
}

function withdraw() external {
    uint256 amount = pendingWithdrawals[msg.sender];
    pendingWithdrawals[msg.sender] = 0;
    (bool ok, ) = msg.sender.call{value: amount}("");
    require(ok, "Withdrawal failed");
}

WHY IT MATTERS: Classic bug: an auction contract that pushes refunds to outbid bidders in a loop -- if ONE bidder is a contract that rejects ETH, the entire refund loop reverts, locking the auction. Pull payments isolate each user's failure from everyone else's.

FURTHER READING:
- Solidity docs, Withdrawal Pattern: https://docs.soliditylang.org/en/latest/common-patterns.html#withdrawal-from-contracts
- ConsenSys, Smart Contract Best Practices: https://consensys.io/blog

