Users can sign a message off-chain with their private key (no gas cost), and a contract can later verify that signature on-chain using ecrecover -- proving the message came from a specific address without needing a transaction to create it.

EXAMPLE (using OpenZeppelin's ECDSA library):
import "@openzeppelin/contracts/utils/cryptography/ECDSA.sol";
using ECDSA for bytes32;

function verify(bytes32 messageHash, bytes memory signature, address signer) public pure returns (bool) {
    bytes32 ethSignedHash = MessageHashUtils.toEthSignedMessageHash(messageHash);
    return ethSignedHash.recover(signature) == signer;
}

WHY IT MATTERS: This pattern powers gasless approvals (permit), off-chain order books, and meta-transactions -- letting users prove intent without paying gas for every action. Always use the audited OpenZeppelin ECDSA library rather than raw ecrecover, which has known signature-malleability pitfalls if handled incorrectly.

FURTHER READING:
- OpenZeppelin ECDSA docs: https://docs.openzeppelin.com/contracts/utilities#cryptography
- Solidity docs, ecrecover: https://docs.soliditylang.org/en/latest/units-and-global-variables.html#mathematical-and-cryptographic-functions

