body:
A flash loan lets you borrow any amount of an asset with zero collateral, as long as you repay it (plus a fee) within the SAME transaction -- if you don't, the entire transaction reverts as if it never happened. This is unique to blockchains' atomic transaction model.

Legit uses: arbitrage, collateral swaps, liquidations. Malicious use: flash loan attacks borrow a huge sum to temporarily manipulate a price oracle or vote weight, exploit that manipulation, then repay the loan -- all in one transaction.

EXAMPLE (conceptual flow):
function executeFlashLoan(uint256 amount) external {
    lender.flashLoan(amount);
    // ... your logic runs here (e.g. arbitrage) ...
    // must repay amount + fee before this function ends, or it all reverts
}

WHY IT MATTERS: Flash loan attacks have caused hundreds of millions in DeFi losses, almost always by manipulating a price that another protocol trusted without safeguards. Never let a single transaction's spot price directly control large financial decisions -- use time-weighted average prices (TWAPs) or trusted oracles instead.

FURTHER READING:
- Aave, Flash Loans docs: https://docs.aave.com/developers/guides/flash-loans
- Consensys Diligence, Flash loan attacks: https://consensys.io/diligence

