block.timestamp is set by the miner/validator producing the block, and can be manipulated within a small window (typically a few seconds). Using it for anything requiring precision or unpredictability is risky.

EXAMPLE (risky):
function isLucky() external view returns (bool) {
    return block.timestamp % 2 == 0; // DON'T use this for randomness
}

SAFER USE (timestamps are fine for coarse-grained deadlines):
uint256 public deadline = block.timestamp + 7 days;

function claim() external {
    require(block.timestamp < deadline, "Deadline passed");
}

WHY IT MATTERS: Using block.timestamp for randomness or exact-second timing is a well-known anti-pattern -- validators have a small amount of leeway to nudge it in their favor. It's perfectly fine for things like 'must claim within a week,' just never for anything an attacker profits from predicting.

