A DoS vulnerability lets an attacker block a contract's normal function for everyone -- often by making a loop unexecutable, or by making one specific account permanently blocked from receiving funds it needs to receive for the contract to proceed.

EXAMPLE (vulnerable -- unbounded loop):
address[] public participants;

function payAll() external {
    for (uint256 i = 0; i < participants.length; i++) {
        payable(participants[i]).transfer(1 ether); // if the array grows huge, this runs out of gas
    }
}

SAFER: use the pull-payment pattern (see that lesson) so each user withdraws individually, with no loop at all.

WHY IT MATTERS: DoS bugs don't steal funds directly, but they can permanently freeze a contract -- which is often just as damaging. Any loop over user-controlled, unbounded data (arrays, mappings you iterate manually) is a red flag during review.

