The Diamond pattern is an advanced proxy pattern that lets a single contract address route to MANY logic contracts ('facets') instead of just one -- solving the 24KB contract size limit and enabling more modular upgrades than a standard single-implementation proxy.

Core idea: a Diamond contract's fallback looks up which facet handles the called function (via a function selector mapping) and delegatecalls to it.

EXAMPLE (conceptual selector lookup):
mapping(bytes4 => address) public facetAddress;

fallback() external payable {
    address facet = facetAddress[msg.sig];
    require(facet != address(0), "Function not found");
    (bool ok, ) = facet.delegatecall(msg.data);
    require(ok);
}

WHY IT MATTERS: Large, complex protocols (like some major DeFi platforms) outgrow a single contract's size limit and use Diamonds to split logic across many facets while keeping one stable contract address for users. It's more complex than a standard proxy, so it's usually reserved for genuinely large systems.

FURTHER READING:
- EIP-2535 spec: https://eips.ethereum.org/EIPS/eip-2535
- Diamond standard reference implementation: https://github.com/mudgen/diamond

