On-chain governance lets token holders propose and vote on changes to a protocol -- parameter updates, treasury spending, even contract upgrades -- typically weighted by how many governance tokens each voter holds.

EXAMPLE (simplified):
struct Proposal {
    string description;
    uint256 voteCount;
    uint256 deadline;
    bool executed;
}

mapping(uint256 => Proposal) public proposals;
mapping(uint256 => mapping(address => bool)) public hasVoted;

function vote(uint256 proposalId) external {
    require(!hasVoted[proposalId][msg.sender], "Already voted");
    hasVoted[proposalId][msg.sender] = true;
    proposals[proposalId].voteCount += token.balanceOf(msg.sender);
}

WHY IT MATTERS: Naive token-weighted voting is vulnerable to flash-loan voting attacks (borrow tokens, vote, return them, all in one transaction) -- which is why most real governance systems use a snapshot of balances at a past block, not the current balance. OpenZeppelin Governor is the standard, audited framework.

