An escrow contract holds funds from one party until a condition is met (delivery confirmed, dispute resolved, time elapsed), then releases them to the appropriate party -- removing the need to trust either side directly.

EXAMPLE (simplified):
enum State { AWAITING_PAYMENT, AWAITING_DELIVERY, COMPLETE }
State public state;
address public buyer;
address public seller;

function confirmDelivery() external {
    require(msg.sender == buyer, "Only buyer");
    require(state == State.AWAITING_DELIVERY);
    state = State.COMPLETE;
    payable(seller).call{value: address(this).balance}("");
}

WHY IT MATTERS: Escrow is one of the clearest real-world use cases for smart contracts -- replacing a trusted third party (like a lawyer holding funds) with code both parties can verify in advance. Real implementations usually add a neutral arbiter for disputes and a timeout/refund path.

FURTHER READING:
- Solidity by Example, Escrow patterns: https://solidity-by-example.org/
- Ethereum.org, Smart contract use cases: https://ethereum.org/en/developers/docs/smart-contracts/

