There's no truly random source natively available on-chain -- block.timestamp, blockhash, and similar values can all be predicted or influenced by miners/validators to some degree. For anything valuable (lotteries, NFT trait assignment, game outcomes), you need a verifiable off-chain randomness source.

EXAMPLE (conceptual Chainlink VRF flow):
function requestRandomWords() external returns (uint256 requestId) {
    requestId = COORDINATOR.requestRandomWords(keyHash, subId, 3, 200000, 1);
}

function fulfillRandomWords(uint256 requestId, uint256[] memory randomWords) internal override {
    uint256 result = randomWords[0] % 100; // use the verified random value
}

WHY IT MATTERS: Contracts that used block.timestamp or blockhash for high-value randomness (like NFT rarity or lottery winners) have had their outcomes predicted and exploited by miners or bots. Chainlink VRF provides cryptographically verifiable randomness that even the oracle itself can't manipulate without detection.

FURTHER READING:
- Chainlink VRF docs: https://docs.chain.link/vrf
- SWC Registry, SWC-120 (weak randomness): https://swcregistry.io/docs/SWC-120

